LLVMSupport: the ABSD OS layer for signals, programs, memory and the
watchdog; no signal handlers, no sigprocmask, no dup2, no getsid

Failure: Unix/Program.inc needs wait4, struct rusage::ru_maxrss and
posix_spawn/fork/execve (compile errors at Program.inc:348,425,472);
Unix/Signals.inc needs sigaction, sigaltstack, dl_iterate_phdr and backtrace;
Unix/Memory.inc needs mmap/munmap/mprotect; Unix/Watchdog.inc needs alarm
(undefined at link: none is in the kit, its POSIX adapter or Picolibc);
CrashRecoveryContext.cpp needs sigaction; Unix/Process.inc needs dup2
(FixupStandardFileDescriptors) and blocks signals with sigprocmask around
every close (SafelyCloseFileDescriptor), which the kit answers ENOSYS, so
every such close would fail; it seeds rand() from /dev/urandom, which ABSD
has not; LockFileManager.cpp calls getsid.
Change: under LLVM_ABSD_KIT, Signals.cpp, Program.cpp, Memory.cpp and
Watchdog.cpp include ABSD/*.inc (cxx/glue/llvm-support, copied in by
scripts/build-llvm-kit.sh) instead of Unix/*.inc: registrations kept,
no handler installed, no stack trace; program execution refused with a
message, Wait reports no child; memory blocks are page-aligned heap blocks,
read+write only, executable or read-only protection refused (ENOTSUP);
watchdog does nothing. CrashRecoveryContext installs no handlers;
FixupStandardFileDescriptors does nothing (the kit never hands 0-2 to
open()); SafelyCloseFileDescriptor is close(); the seed comes from the kit's
getentropy (RDRAND) first; LockFileManager assumes a lock owner is alive.
Behaviour preserved: every other platform includes the same Unix/*.inc and
code as before. On ABSD the behaviour is what the kit's contract allows:
nothing is emulated (no fake mmap, no fake spawn, no fake signal).
Upstreamability: as an LLVM OS port (lib/Support/ABSD/ next to Unix/ and
Windows/, keyed on a target OS macro) it is the shape upstream uses for
Windows; not upstreamable before ABSD is a known OS in LLVM's triple.

